 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Relates to applications for child care assistance; authorizes the use of a simplified application form for persons applying only for child care assistance.",
 "title": "Relates to applications for child care assistance",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A4469",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A4469-2015",
     "printNo": "A4469",
     "session": 2015,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 226\n\nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n\nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n\nAssembly Bill Number 4469, entitled:\n\n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend the social services law, in relation to applica-\n      tions for child care assistance\"\n\n    NOT APPROVED\n\n  This bill would amend the Social Services Law to require Local  Social\nServices  Districts (LSSDs) to develop and utilize an application solely\nfor child care assistance. It would also require the Office of Temporary\nDisability Assistance (OTDA) and  the  Office  of  Children  and  Family\nServices  (OCFS)  to  link their websites to such applications for their\nrespective LSSDs.\n\n  Simplifying the application process for child care  assistance  is  an\nimportant  policy  goal.  However,  requiring an individual to apply for\nchild care assistance using a specific application, rather than as  part\nof  a  common  application that can be used to determine eligibility for\nother services, may be detrimental to those who could benefit from other\nservices they might receive if they filled out the  common  application.\nAccordingly,  this may compromise an LSSD's ability to provide necessary\nassistance.\n\n  Moreover, while all LSSDs are required to process manually  their  own\nchild  care applications, many LSSDs cannot currently accept and process\non-line applications. Requiring LSSDs to do  so,  without  appropriating\nany  funds,  would  impose  an unnecessary burden on municipalities, and\nshould therefore be addressed in the upcoming State budget negotiations.\nFor these reasons, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n\n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n\n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 226,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2015
   }
